**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4"]}
E0001 is the formal authority baseline for Abiy’s command and accountability context.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia frees opposition leaders from prison, announces political dialogue","publisher":"Reuters / Euronews","publication_date":"2022-01-08","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/01/08/us-ethiopia-politics","claim":"The government freed several opposition leaders and said it would begin political dialogue after 14 months of war and arrests.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on a concrete release and dialogue announcement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to early-year restraint.","ruler_attribution":"Government action under Abiy’s premiership; it shows capacity for release and dialogue, though not the fate of all detainees.","contrary_evidence":["This is a limited positive step amid broader repression.","It does not show durable reform or broader protection."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia's cabinet approves lifting of state of emergency","publisher":"Reuters / Euronews","publication_date":"2022-01-27","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/01/27/us-ethiopia-conflict","claim":"Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed's office said regular law enforcement could now neutralize threats, and the cabinet approved ending the nationwide state of emergency early.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report quoting the PM office on a formal legal change.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the federal executive under Abiy, but it reflects a partial rollback of emergency powers rather than a full safety restoration.","contrary_evidence":["The state of emergency had already enabled mass arrests and detention before the rollback.","Ending the emergency did not end repression in other regions."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.6","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia arrests former government officials from Tigray, rights body says","publisher":"Reuters / Euronews","publication_date":"2022-03-19","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/19/us-ethiopia-conflict-arrests","claim":"Ethiopian authorities detained at least eight senior former officials from Tigray, according to the Ethiopian Human Rights Commission head.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report quoting the state-appointed human rights commission.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to federal authorities during Abiy’s premiership; it shows targeted detention of political/ethnic elites.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not specify the legal basis or whether all detentions were centrally ordered.","It is one incident, though part of a larger pattern."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rights Commission confirms OLF leaders detained against due process, subjected to abuse","publisher":"Addis Standard","publication_date":"2022-05-19","url":"https://addisstandard.com/news-rights-commission-confirms-olf-leaders-detained-against-due-process-subjected-to-abuse/","claim":"The Ethiopian Human Rights Commission said several OLF leaders and members were detained without due process and asked for their immediate release and investigation of security officers who beat prisoners.","locator":"Article body / lead","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local independent reporting on an official domestic rights-body finding, with direct attribution to EHRC monitoring.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Abiy because the detentions occurred under the federal political environment and involved security actors; the article does not prove personal ordering of each abuse.","contrary_evidence":["The abuses were carried out by regional security actors, so national attribution is mediated.","It is strongest for due process failure, weaker for direct presidential/PM incitement."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Journalists Yayesew Shimelis and Temesgen Desalegn detained in Ethiopia","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-05-27","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/05/journalists-yayesew-shimelis-and-temesgen-desalegn-detained-in-ethiopia/","claim":"CPJ said Ethiopian authorities detained two journalists and accused them of crimes against the state while police did not file formal charges or specify the laws allegedly broken.","locator":"Article body","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized press-freedom watchdog with contemporaneous case documentation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the federal crackdown climate under Abiy’s government, though the arrests were executed by police and framed as criminal enforcement.","contrary_evidence":["Police claimed the journalists committed crimes, but CPJ says formal charges and specificity were lacking at the time.","This is press-safety evidence, not a full measure of all domestic violence."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"International Commission of Human Rights Experts on Ethiopia 30 June 2022","publisher":"United Nations Office at Geneva / OHCHR","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.unognewsroom.org/story/en/1206/international-commission-of-human-rights-experts-on-ethiopia-30-june-2022/3662","claim":"The UN commission said violations and abuses were still being perpetrated with impunity, and it found reasonable grounds to believe the federal government and allies committed crimes against humanity in Tigray, including starvation as a method of warfare and a drone strike that killed civilians at an IDP camp.","locator":"Story text summarizing the Human Rights Council briefing","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN summary of a commission briefing with specific atrocity findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and central to domestic protection and accountability.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to federal government forces and allies under Abiy’s premiership, though not every battlefield act is personally ordered by him.","contrary_evidence":["This is an evidentiary finding, not a criminal conviction.","It concerns conflict-related atrocities rather than routine policing."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Ethiopia","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/ethiopia","claim":"HRW said authorities maintained an effective siege on Tigray through 2022, kept banking, electricity, communications and humanitarian access shut for long stretches, and that journalists, civil society, and outspoken figures faced a more hostile and restrictive environment.","locator":"Ethiopia chapter, conflict and expression sections","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual rights synthesis covering the full 2022 period with concrete chronology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but explicitly about events of 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Abiy because the siege and restrictions were government-side choices and part of the federal domestic-safety environment.","contrary_evidence":["This is a retrospective synthesis, not a single contemporaneous incident report.","It blends federal and non-federal responsibilities."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.6","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia’s Invisible Ethnic Cleansing","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-06-16","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/06/16/ethiopias-invisible-ethnic-cleansing","claim":"HRW said Ethiopian authorities had severely restricted access and independent scrutiny in western Tigray while federal forces acquiesced in or possibly participated in a campaign that forcibly expelled several hundred thousand Tigrayans and kept men in overcrowded detention sites where many died of disease, starvation, or torture.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights report with detailed interviews and attribution language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and highly relevant to vulnerable-group protection and detention.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the federal government’s wartime and security environment under Abiy; the report is careful that some abuses were carried out by regional actors with federal acquiescence or possible participation.","contrary_evidence":["The report emphasizes restricted access, so some details are hard to independently verify.","It is strongest for Western Tigray, not all of Ethiopia."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia: Civilians in Western Oromia Left Unprotected","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-08-31","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/08/31/ethiopia-civilians-western-oromia-left-unprotected","claim":"HRW said an armed group killed about 400 Amhara civilians in western Oromia on June 18, 2022, and that Ethiopian security forces nearby did not arrive for hours and failed to protect residents adequately.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed contemporaneous field report with quantified civilian harm and explicit protection failure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the federal government's failure to protect civilians under Abiy’s premiership, though the attackers were an armed group rather than the state itself.","contrary_evidence":["The killings were committed by non-state attackers, so state responsibility is failure to protect rather than direct execution.","The article does not settle every aspect of operational command at the scene."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopian government accused of deadly air strikes on Oromiya region","publisher":"Reuters / Euronews","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/28/ethiopia-conflict","claim":"Reuters reported that two Oromiya organizations accused the Ethiopian army of conducting air strikes that killed hundreds of civilians just as peace talks were about to start.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report on civilian harm allegations in an Ethiopian region.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the federal military under Abiy’s government if the allegations are accurate; the article itself presents the claim as an accusation rather than a proven fact.","contrary_evidence":["Government and army spokespeople did not respond in the article.","The source is allegation-heavy and should be paired with independent corroboration."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopian women, children bear brunt of pandemic, conflicts, drought induced basic rights violation: Rights Commission","publisher":"Addis Standard","publication_date":"2022-11-01","url":"https://addisstandard.com/news-ethiopian-women-children-bear-brunt-of-pandemic-conflicts-drought-induced-basic-rights-violation-rights-commission/","claim":"The Ethiopian Human Rights Commission said women and children had been exposed to heightened violations of basic rights due to conflict, drought, Covid-19, and lack of protection, and that women faced sexual violence and discrimination.","locator":"Article body summarizing the EHRC report","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Domestic reporting on an official national human-rights institution’s findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Abiy because the reported failures in protection occurred under the federal government and its regional counterparts.","contrary_evidence":["This is a summary of a rights body report rather than the report itself.","It aggregates multiple causes, so attribution is partly indirect."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Looting, forced removals plague Ethiopia's Tigray despite truce - witnesses","publisher":"Reuters / Euronews","publication_date":"2022-12-03","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/12/03/ethiopia-conflict-truce","claim":"Reuters reported that Ethiopia's allies were looting towns, arresting and killing civilians, and relocating thousands of people from a disputed part of Tigray despite the November 2 truce.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report on post-truce abuses and displacement claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and after the ceasefire.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Abiy because it tests whether the federal side could restrain allied forces and protect civilians after signing the truce; the article notes Eritrean and Amhara forces were not parties to the ceasefire.","contrary_evidence":["The article centers witness and aid-worker claims, not a formal investigation.","Some actors were outside the ceasefire, so attribution is partly indirect."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across Reuters/Euronews, CPJ, HRW, UN/OHCHR, EHRC-linked local reporting, Amnesty, and Freedom House.

Kept:
- Two early-2022 restraint signals: opposition releases / dialogue and the early lifting of the state of emergency.
- One targeted detention report on Tigrayan officials.
- One local rights-body finding on OLF due-process abuse.
- One CPJ detention alert for journalists.
- One UN commission summary on ongoing atrocities, impunity, starvation, and a civilian-killing drone strike.
- One broad HRW synthesis of 2022 conditions.
- One HRW field report on Western Tigray abuses and access restrictions.
- One HRW field report on western Oromia civilian protection failure.
- One Reuters report on alleged deadly airstrikes in Oromiya.
- One local report on EHRC findings about women and children.
- One Reuters report on post-truce looting / forced removals.

Rejected or downweighted:
- Duplicate Reuters syndications of the same arrest/detention stories.
- 2020-2021 background reports unless they were needed for inherited context, since the chapter is 2022.
- 2023-2025 pages on corridor evictions and later state-of-emergency crises because they were out of period.
- Advocacy pages that repeated the same claim without adding new chronology, attribution, or evidence type.
- Purely general press-freedom or human-rights landing pages that did not add a specific 2022 claim.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct proof of Abiy’s personal ordering or awareness for some regional security-force abuses is still limited; much attribution remains governmental or command-level rather than personally signed.
- The balance of responsibility between federal, regional, and allied forces remains contested in some violence outside Tigray, especially Oromia and post-truce incidents.
- I did not find a single definitive domestic accountability mechanism in 2022 that credibly remedied the abuses; the record points more to partial release/rollback than durable oversight.
- More local-language court, commission, or parliamentary material could sharpen attribution, but it is unlikely to change the overall 2022 pattern: partial early restraint, followed by recurring, multi-region repression and serious civilian-protection failures.

Severity-wise, the chapter evidence lands in recurring to widespread repression with systematic features in the Tigray detention/access domain, but not a mass-terror floor.
